ef 17592186045417] [17592186520441 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186520206 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186520441 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186520215 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186520441 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045494 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186520441 #edb/ref 1077 "field-3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186520441 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186520135 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186520441 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "f45ede74-48fb-52d3-a7d6-85c98396d7ba" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186520445 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186520504 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186520445 #edb/ref 1039 "Comparing Two Events by Likelihood" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186520445 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186520465 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186520445 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "05b40dd4-a99b-5d5e-93b4-767ed99d62a0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186520465 #edb/ref 1069 "e-24942" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186520465 #edb/ref 1071 "A scientist records how long it takes for a chemical reaction to complete. The experiment is conducted $40$ times. The table below shows the results.\n\n| Time (seconds) | Frequency |\n| --- | --- |\n| $<10$ | $6$ |\n| $10-20$ | $14$ |\n| $20-30$ | $8$ |\n| $30-40$ | $7$ |\n| $>40$ | $5$ |\n\nBased on the experimental probabilities of these results, complete the following statements.\n\n$\\qquad$A reaction completing in more than $30$ seconds is\n$\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{\\phantom{\\text{unlikely}}}.$\n\n$\\qquad$A reaction completing in less than $20$ seconds is\n$\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{\\phantom{\\text{as likely as not}}}.$\n\n$\\qquad$A reaction completing in more than $30$ seconds is\n$\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{\\phantom{\\text{less likely than}}}$\na reaction completing in less than $20$ seconds." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186520465 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4ced75f7-e2c9-50f2-b2cf-114222fc960d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186520465 #edb/ref 1074 "One event is more likely than another if it has a greater probability of occurring. If two events have the same probability, they are equally likely.\n\nLet's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- To compute the experimental probability of a reaction completing in more than $30$ seconds, we divide the number of times a reaction took more than $30$ seconds $(7+5=12)$ by the total number of trials $(40).$ This gives\n![](images/41/41ae3b0ee5b77a5967ee949abfd5dc5327fc840e9061cecf1dfd52728405415f.svg)\n\nThis probability is a small number, closer to $0.$ Since\n\n$$\n0 < \\dfrac{3}{10} < \\dfrac12,\n$$\n\nthis event is $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{\\text{unlikely}}.$\n\n- To compute the experimental probability of a reaction completing in less than $20$ seconds, we divide the number of times a reaction took less than $20$ seconds $(6+14=20)$ by the total number of trials $(40).$ This gives\n![](images/19/194542c8095442fe631a733e681d43b025ac5b5f7f478f7b3c541e3c51718c69.svg)\n\nSince the probability is $\\dfrac12,$ this event is $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{\\text{as likely as not}}.$\n\n- The experimental probability of a reaction completing in more than $30$ seconds is less than the experimental probability of a reaction completing in less than $20$ seconds:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{10} < \\dfrac12.\n$$\n\nTherefore, a reaction completing in more than $30$ seconds is\n$\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{\\text{less likely than}}$\na reaction completing in less than $20$ seconds." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186520504 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186520413 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186520504 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186520422 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186520504 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186520441 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186520504 #edb/ref 1069 "q-360663" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186520504 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186520504 #edb/ref 1071 "Sarah conducts an experiment $40$ times to see how quickly a machine sorts a bin of parts. The table below shows the results of her experiments.\n\n| Time (seconds) | Frequency |\n| --- | --- |\n| $<20$ | $6$ |\n| $20-30$ | $16$ |\n| $30-40$ | $10$ |\n| $40-50$ | $5$ |\n| $>50$ | $3$ |\n\nBased on the experimental probabilities of these results, complete the following statements.\n\n$\\,$A machine sorting a bin of parts in less than $30$ seconds is\n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$A machine sorting a bin of parts in more than $30$ seconds is\n{{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$A machine sorting a bin of parts in less than $30$ seconds is\n{{field-3}}\na machine sorting a bin of parts in more than $30$ seconds." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186520504 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e4735ca8-e709-5697-8aaf-f9b52ab3104d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186520504 #edb/ref 1074 "One event is more likely than another if it has a greater probability of occurring. If two events have the same probability, they are equally likely.\n\nLet's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- To compute the experimental probability of a machine sorting a bin of parts in less than $30$ seconds, we divide the number of times a machine sorted a bin of parts in less than $30$ seconds $(6+16=22)$ by the total number of times Sarah conducted the experiment $(40).$ This gives\n\n$$\nP(\\text{sorts in }<30\\text{ seconds})=\\frac{22}{40}.\n$$\n\nThis probability is a relatively large number, closer to $1.$ Since\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}<\\frac{22}{40}<1,\n$$\n\nthis event is $\\text{likely}.$\n\n- To compute the experimental probability of a machine sorting a bin of parts in more than $30$ seconds, we divide the number of times a machine sorted a bin of parts in more than $30$ seconds $(10+5+3=18)$ by the total number of times Sarah conducted the experiment $(40).$ This gives\n\n$$\nP(\\text{sorts in }>30\\text{ seconds})=\\frac{18}{40}.\n$$\n\nThis probability is a small number, closer to $0.$ Since\n\n$$\n0<\\frac{18}{40}<\\frac{1}{2},\n$$\n\nthis event is $\\text{unlikely}.$\n\n- The experimental probability of a machine sorting a bin of parts in less than $30$ seconds is *greater than* the experimental probability of a machine sorting a bin of parts in more than $30$ seconds:\n\n$$\nP(\\text{sorts in }<30\\text{ seconds})=\\frac{22}{40}>\\frac{18}{40}=P(\\text{sorts in }>30\\text{ seconds})\n$$\n\nTherefore, a machine sorting a bin of parts in less than $30$ seconds is\n$\\text{more likely than}$\na machine sorting a bin of parts in more than $30$ seconds." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186520519 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "01cfa563-e8bf-5304-95ca-8bb9735ba5e0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186520519 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186512877 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186520520 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "05b93622-e0e7-508b-9fee-2ccaa8ca47d3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186520520 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186234280 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186520521 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "0628f9ee-2554-5fa8-8aa0-10a3d900487f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186520521 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186512917 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186520522 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "0d2cb728-c445-559a-a95d-812914b99b3c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186520522 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186520504 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186520523 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "0f34f87b-f7ac-527a-b09f-2cc09b251507" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186520523 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186234141 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186520524 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "0f861d49-4d23-53e3-86b6-0bee29fcb3ce" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186520524 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186234105 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186520527 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "1297bf20-7c0b-58a5-af3a-7cfb5196c4e0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186520527 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186512898 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186520529 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "16012bb6-e45d-513e-ad56-d36da45179d6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186520529 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186512892 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186520530 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "170287ae-6085-53d8-84e4-7ffa3eb28101" #edb/ref 1